 ### [Hybrid Ray-Casting](3180-hybrid-ray-casting.md)
 A ray-casting variant that produces an intermediate image aligned with one of the volume faces and then transforms it to the view direction to achieve a one-to-one mapping of ray-samples onto the volume. It transforms the volume into an intermediate coordinate system (shear and scale for perspective), generates the intermediate image, and warps it onto the view plane.
 
+### [Single-pass Raycasting](6121-single-pass-raycasting.md)
+Single-pass raycasting is a direct volume rendering technique that generates one ray per pixel, sampling a 3D texture along the ray and compositing the results in a single GPU rendering pass to produce a 2D image. It distinguishes itself from multi-pass approaches by avoiding temporary framebuffers for intermediate ray results, thereby addressing the low efficiency of multi-pass pipelines on modern graphics hardware.
+
+### [Object-Order Ray-Casting](6204-object-order-ray-casting.md)
+Object-order ray-casting is a volume rendering technique that transforms the volumetric data to align with the view direction, allowing rays to sample at integer grid points. It combines the high-quality trilinear interpolation of image-order ray-casting with the cache-friendly, regular memory access patterns of object-order methods. Unlike image-order ray-casting, which suffers from cache misses due to irregular voxel access, or Shear-Warp, which introduces artifacts and blurring, this method maintains interactive frame rates while preserving image quality.
+
+### [RaycastBrightnessControl](6526-raycastbrightnesscontrol.md)
+RaycastBrightnessControl is a back-to-front direct volume raycasting technique that modulates voxel brightness based on a threshold parameter. It differs from front-to-back methods by rendering back-facing polygons to start sampling from the furthest voxels, and from density-based variants by controlling luminance rather than opacity accumulation or color bias.
+
+### [Volume Raycasting Pipeline](8376-volume-raycasting-pipeline.md)
+The goal of raycasting is the (approximate) calculation of the volume rendering integral for every viewing ray originating at the image plane and passing through the volume. It structures the rendering process into discrete stages: ray generation, volume intersection, sampling, classification, shading, and compositing, enabling modular optimization and hardware acceleration.
+
+### Backward Mapping (Ray Tracing)
+A volumetric rendering technique that uses computer graphics ray-tracing to form the image. For each pixel, a ray is projected through the data volume, and intersecting voxels influence the pixel value [S45, unofficial].
